1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,200
MyCampusView Academy. Robotics 101, video 6: Building the robot body.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,201
Time to build.

3
00:00:06,251 --> 00:00:12,212
Clear your table, keep the screws in a small bowl, and ask

4
00:00:12,262 --> 00:00:16,150
an adult to help with the screwdriver.

5
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:19,737
Here is the plan from above.

6
00:00:19,787 --> 00:00:22,937
The two motors go at the back.

7
00:00:22,987 --> 00:00:26,030
The castor goes at the front.

8
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:31,150
The sensors sit at the very front, looking down.

9
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:35,586
First fix both motors with their brackets.

10
00:00:35,636 --> 00:00:38,508
Push the wheels onto the shafts.

11
00:00:38,558 --> 00:00:41,522
Screw the castor under the front.

12
00:00:41,572 --> 00:00:46,910
Mount the Arduino and the motor driver on top, then fix the

13
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:49,650
battery holder and the switch.

14
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:56,015
Mount the two sensors at the front, facing down, about five to

15
00:00:56,065 --> 00:00:58,990
ten millimetres above the floor.

16
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:04,476
Space them a little wider than your black line, so the line

17
00:01:04,526 --> 00:01:06,150
fits between them.

18
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:13,896
If the sensors are too close together, both see the line at once.

19
00:01:13,946 --> 00:01:21,593
If they are too far apart, the robot wobbles before it notices a turn.

20
00:01:21,643 --> 00:01:26,650
A gap a little wider than the line works well.

21
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:33,658
Before you wire anything, check that the wheels spin freely, the robot

22
00:01:33,708 --> 00:01:39,150
sits level, nothing is loose, and the battery switch is off.

23
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:46,000
Next: Wiring the electronics safely.
